Awesome, Pankaj! Here's a complete working solution to automatically export your D365 F&O model code from a Tier-1 VM and push it to an Azure DevOps Git repository via CI.


---

✅ What You’ll Get:

A PowerShell script that copies your custom model from D365's local packages folder

Pushes it to a new branch in your Azure DevOps Git repo

A Classic Pipeline setup or YAML option to run this on schedule, after a PR merge, or manually



---

🎯 Prerequisites (Once Only)

1. ✅ Self-Hosted Agent on Tier-1 DevBox

Download agent:
Azure DevOps > Project Settings > Agent Pools > New Agent

Install on your Tier-1 Dev VM

Make sure the agent:

Runs as a service

Can access K:\AosService\PackagesLocalDirectory

Has Git installed

Has permissions to push to your Azure DevOps Git repo




---

2. ✅ Create a Personal Access Token (PAT)

You need a Git push PAT for authentication:

Go to: User Settings > Personal Access Tokens

Scope: Code (Read & Write)

Save the token securely (you’ll add it to pipeline secrets)



---

📝 Step 1: PowerShell Script (export-d365-model.ps1)

# VARIABLES
$repoUrl = "https://<yourorg>@dev.azure.com/<yourorg>/<project>/_git/<repo>"  # use PAT format if needed
$patToken = "$(GitPAT)"  # Secure variable in pipeline
$modelName = "MyModel"   # Replace with your model name
$modelSource = "K:\AosService\PackagesLocalDirectory\$modelName"
$branch = "model-export-$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd-HHmmss')"
$tempDir = "C:\Git\D365ModelTemp"

# CLEAN TEMP
if (Test-Path $tempDir) { Remove-Item -Recurse -Force $tempDir }
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path $tempDir | Out-Null

# COPY MODEL FILES
Copy-Item -Recurse -Path "$modelSource\*" -Destination $tempDir

# INIT GIT
Set-Location $tempDir
git init
git config user.email "azure-pipelines@dev.azure.com"
git config user.name "Azure Pipelines"

# AUTHENTICATE USING PAT
$repoWithAuth = $repoUrl -replace "https://", "https://:$patToken@"

git remote add origin $repoWithAuth
git checkout -b $branch
git add .
git commit -m "Exported $modelName from D365 on $(Get-Date)"
git push origin $branch


---

🧪 Step 2: Classic Pipeline Setup

🎯 Trigger: after PR merged or manual

1. Go to Pipelines > New pipeline > Classic Editor


2. Select your Azure Repos Git > "Start with an Empty Job"


3. Agent Pool: Select the Tier-1 self-hosted agent


4. Add a PowerShell Task

Script path: path to export-d365-model.ps1

Arguments: none




🛡 Secrets & Variables

1. Go to the "Variables" tab


2. Add a secret variable:

Name: GitPAT

Value: your personal access token

Mark it as secret





---

💡 Optional: Trigger After PR Merge

If you want this to run only when code is merged to main:

Go to Triggers tab

Enable "Enable continuous integration"

Add branch filter: main



---

✅ Final Result

Every time you run this:

It copies the current model code

Creates a timestamped Git branch

Pushes that branch to your Azure DevOps Git repo



